    thinking: "**Analyzing User Intent**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the user\'s request. It\'s about how visual elements in the \"treatments\" section should animate during scrolling. Specifically, I\'m focusing on that \"vanishing\" effect\342\200\224making images and videos seem to scroll into one another. The goal is a seamless visual transition for the user.\n\n\n**Designing Visual Transitions**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the technical implementation. In `TreatmentsSection.tsx`, I\'m evaluating lines 433-457, specifically focusing on the existing vertical stacking of media. The current implementation doesn\'t create that desired \"vanishing\" effect. My goal is to add a dynamic experience where images/videos visually recede into one another, achieving that high-fashion editorial feel.\n\n\n**Evaluating Architectural Options**\n\nI\'m now comparing two architectural options for the sticky deck, scroll-driven vanishing panels. Option A involves a sticky deck with CSS positioning and scroll-driven transforms; the initial panel pins in place, and as the user continues scrolling, subsequent panels overlap the previous one. Meanwhile, the first panel scales down and fades into the background.\n\n\n**Tracing Visual Geometry**\n\nMy current focus is on the intricate interplay of visual elements. I\'m exploring the geometry of a `flex-col` layout where sticky panels overlap during scroll. I\'m particularly interested in how each panel\'s `height` and `top` positioning affects the \"vanishing\" effect. I\'m visualizing how Panel 0, pinned at `top: 6.5rem`, interacts with its sibling, Panel 1, and the resulting visual flow. It seems like the immediate overlap is perfect!\n\n\n**Comparing Sticky Solutions**\n\nI\'m now comparing native sticky cards versus a single sticky stage approach. I\'m visualizing a tall scroll track and a pinned viewport on the right, aiming for synchronized scrolling across modalities. This setup creates a seamless, side-by-side scrolling effect, with the media stage remaining gracefully pinned at the same vertical height as the left column throughout the section.\n\n\n**Visualizing Dynamic Flow**\n\nI\'m now picturing the precise choreography of the upcoming visual. The next media asset, like a \"nails\" video, will elegantly rise into view with a clip-path and subtle parallax. Simultaneously, the existing \"facials\" video will shrink, fade, blur, and drift inward, essentially vanishing as the new content seamlessly takes its place. This is where I\'m aiming for the true vanishing effect. The incoming content will scale up into the space with a soft gradient mask. The result should look great!\n\n\n**Refining Transition Logic**\n\nI\'m now comparing the transition between the \"Skincare Consultations\" and \"Pre & Aftercare\" sections. I\'ve realized a single sticky viewport offers a cleaner transition. With a single sticky stage, the modalities spread unpin together seamlessly, avoiding any whitespace gaps. Stacked sticky cards present a slight drawback: the incoming content is visible before it fully enters. I\'m re-evaluating both options to ensure I understand the user\'s intent regarding the scrolling behavior in the \"treatments\" section.\n\n\n**Defining Vanishing Logic**\n\nI\'m now precisely defining the \"vanishing\" effect, breaking it into phases. The goal is a seamless transition between media assets as the user scrolls. I\'m focusing on the technical parameters\342\200\224opacity, scale, blur, and clip-path\342\200\224to achieve a smooth, integrated experience. The \"vanishing\" isn\'t a simple fade, it\'s a dynamic visual progression that makes the assets feel like they scroll into one another.\n\n\n**Implementing Visual Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the specific parameters for the \"vanishing\" effect, now that I have a clear idea. I\'ve broken down the steps of the transition into distinct visual changes. The old element fades, scales, blurs, and shifts upward. The new element comes into view with a blend mask, clip-path, and subtle motion to create a fluid, layered effect. I am evaluating how the incoming slide should integrate into the scene.\n\n**Envisioning Visual Melting**\n\nI\'m now picturing the precise vanishing act. Imagine the \"Facials\" video receding, drifting up, blurring, and fading, all while the \"Nail Art\" video gracefully rises into the same frame. It\'s about achieving that seamless, melting visual transition. The new video de-zooms slightly as it replaces the old, like one is vanishing right into the next. I\'m focusing on the choreography of these elements. I\'m thinking about a minimal editorial progress indicator.\n\n\n**Refining Display Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how to display this on smaller devices. On mobile, I\'m considering a different layout: hiding the right column and showing each visual inline below the corresponding chapter, including a fade/scale transition during switching. This maintains a clean and luxurious aesthetic, keeping the media frame unboxed and borderless. I am envisioning a potential super subtle vertical indicator, perhaps as small as 01\342\200\22304. I\'m prioritizing minimal UI elements.\n\n\n**Calculating Visual States**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating the visual states for each slide during the transition. I\'m normalizing the progress to a 0-1 range for a clean transition. I am planning on calculating the active index and a per-slide visual state, including opacity, scale, and translateYPercent to achieve the vanishing effect. The index, progress, and visual state parameters must be linked to achieve synchronized visual effects.\n\n**Rendering Synchronized Panels**\n\nI\'m now focusing on synchronizing the visual effects across panels. I need to calculate the active slide and set visual parameters. I am calculating `activeIndex`, and `slides`, a per-slide visual state including opacity, scale, translateYPercent, clipTopPercent, blurPx and featherProgress. I have an ordered list of transitions that I am considering.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Media Visuals**\n\nI\'m now determining how to make sure that the index, progress, and visual states work together. I am calculating `activeIndex`, and `slides`, per-slide visual state including opacity, scale, translateYPercent, clipTopPercent, blurPx and featherProgress. The visual parameters, will sync and render to create the \"vanishing\" effect, which is very smooth. I am considering the use of `zIndex` and `isVisible` for optimum performance. I\'m focusing on scroll behavior.\n\n\n**Verifying Scroll Calculations**\n\nI\'m now verifying my calculations for scroll travel distance. I\'m focusing on the relationship between `trackHeight`, `stickyStageHeight`, and `stickyTopOffset`. I\'m using an example to confirm that when the scroll position aligns, the sticky stage pins at the correct moment and unpins smoothly. Specifically, at `scrollY = 1904`, the stage pins. Then at `scrollY = 4604` (`1904 + travel`), the stage is fully unpinned and scrolls away.\n\n\n**Verifying Vanishing Transitions**\n\nI\'m now verifying the \"vanishing\" calculations for smooth transitions between slides. With four slides, at `p = 0` (start), slide 0 is crisp. At `p = 1/3` (progress 0.33), slide 1 is crisp, and slide 0 vanishes. I\'m testing `p = 0.20`, to confirm the parameters for the transition are correctly applied. The vanishing slide should be `opacity 0.45, scale 0.93, blur 4.8px, translateY -3.2%`, while the appearing slide is nearly ready to be displayed.\n\n\n**Validating Sticky Behavior**\n\nI\'m now focusing on verifying the scroll and stick behavior. I\'m confirming that the calculations are working. I am now confident that I can seamlessly scroll up and down, and un-vanish the previous video/image. The transitions are deterministic and symmetric. I\'m verifying that ancestors are not breaking `position: sticky`.\n\n\n"
