  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Inspecting Implementation**\n\nI\'m currently focused on implementing robust unit tests for `resolveModalityDeckState` and `resolveModalityTargetScrollY` within `/src/utils/treatmentsScroll.test.ts`. Concurrently, I\'m updating `/src/components/TreatmentsSection.tsx` to integrate the pinned vanishing deck on the right-hand side. The next step is a deep inspection of the layout.\n\n\n**Analyzing Layout Integrity**\n\nI\'m deep in the weeds, analyzing `TreatmentsSection.tsx`, particularly lines 70\342\200\223210 and 320\342\200\223465. I\'ve uncovered a critical CSS detail related to sticky elements within the grid layout. Specifically, the use of `items-start` on the grid container is preventing proper sticky behavior for the vanishing deck on the right. Switching to `items-stretch` seems like the correct move. This will ensure both columns behave correctly with any future updates.\n\n\n**Revising Sticky Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the sticky behavior within the CSS Grid. The fundamental issue I see is that because the left column itself is the sticky element inside the grid cell, and the grid row height is determined by the tallest column. If the grid row height is `320vh`, then the left column stays pinned throughout that track. The right column wrapper\'s height is now being calculated. It has a style of `354vh`, for the 4 categories, which creates the needed smooth pinned scroll travel.\n\n\n**Optimizing Visual Transition**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the visual transition logic for the editorial cinema frame. I\'m focusing on the effect when images/videos dissolve. My current focus is on the right column\'s wrapper and the sticky editorial cinema frame nested within it. The key lies in creating a seamless transition as images fade. The page\'s alabaster color will be used for the sticky stage background. As an image vanishes, it literally dissolves, due to identical colors. I\'m experimenting with radial gradients to eliminate any jarring black flashes, making the vanishing effect more seamless.\n\n\n**Visualizing the Dissolve**\n\nI\'m now refining the visual dissolve. The vanishing element now fades directly into the alabaster page background, while the incoming image emerges through a soft, dissolving mist. I\'m aiming for a seamless transition, avoiding any jarring black flashes. The overlap timing will ensure both visuals intertwine during the peak transition, preventing blank gaps. For `u = 0.5`, the fading image has an opacity of `0.55`, while the new image emerges with an opacity of `0.70`, a clipTopPercent of 50%, and featherBand of 22%.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Category Updates**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `activeCategoryId` stays synchronized across desktop and mobile. On desktop, scroll-based updates compute `deckProgress` and derive `activeCategoryId`. I\'m verifying the scroll sync and updating on desktop and mobile. I\'m focusing on the effect when a user clicks the category on desktop, and calls `handleSelectCategory`, computing `targetY` and calling `window.scrollTo`. The seamless transition between scrolling and clicking is now the focus. The overlap timing is perfect!\n\n\n**Refining Scroll Synchronization**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction between desktop clicks and smooth scrolling, especially when `prefersReducedMotion` is false. The challenge is ensuring the `activeCategoryId` updates without flickering as the page glides to the target. I\'m exploring two approaches: implementing a programmatic-scroll lock, or directly updating `activeCategoryId` and letting the scroll settle. I\'m also confirming the scroll sync and update on desktop and mobile through unit tests and component updates. Finally, I\'m verifying the mobile behavior, where clicks directly toggle `activeCategoryId` without scroll overrides.\n\n\n"
