  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing UI Immersiveness**\n\nI\'m taking a close look at `BookingDrawer.tsx`. It\'s clear now why the user suggested enhancing immersion. The current implementation is a stark, narrow side panel featuring a series of eight stacked gray HTML form elements. These include basic dropdowns and input fields, lacking visual interest.\n\n\n**Designing Immersive Experience**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the architectural design phase of transforming the `BookingDrawer.tsx` component. The objective is clear: to craft a full-viewport, split-sanctuary experience that feels like stepping into a luxury beauty salon consultation. The design focuses on an immersive modal entrance with a smooth visual transition, locking the body scroll and enabling escape key close.\n\n\n**Visualizing Atmospheric Rituals**\n\nI\'m now generating initial visual concepts for the left-hand section. It\'ll be a full-bleed, edge-to-edge live canvas with videos and photography, dynamically changing with service category selection. A moody editorial vignette ensures text legibility. In the top-left, the NOVA brand wordmark and a location eyebrow will be featured. Down below, there will be a real-time updating dossier card for the chosen ritual.\n\n\n**Detailing Consultation Elements**\n\nI\'m now detailing the right-half consultation flow, focusing on the 3-step process. I\'m prioritizing an architectural header bar with a progress indicator and a minimal close button, as well as an interactive flow with no raw dropdowns. It\'s about designing an ultra-tactile experience, focusing on the Signature Facial as the test case, displaying its duration, cost, and a poetic summary, alongside the subtle bulleted inclusions. The goal is to make the user *feel* the treatment.\n\n\n**Refining Interaction Flow**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the consultation\'s interactive flow, focusing on a compelling selection experience. I\'m finalizing the design for Category Selector Tabs, aiming for crisp architectural hairline buttons to replace chunky pills. These tabs will crossfade the left full-bleed salon film instantly upon selection. I\'m also refining the Tactile Editorial Ritual Cards, focusing on unboxed, hairline-separated rows for a refined selection experience.\n\n\n**Implementing Time Selection**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on enhancing the time selection process within the consultation flow, integrating a custom date picker alongside the existing date input field. I\'m prioritizing a 3-column architectural slot grid for available time slots, ensuring compatibility with the existing unit tests in `bookingLogic.test.ts`. This involves a navigation row for a smoother flow. I\'m also preparing for the third step, which is refining the inputs to enhance the experience.\n\n\n**Finalizing Booking Confirmation**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user\'s information inputs within the consultation. I\'m focusing on Anya Sharma, designing fields for email, phone, and optional skin sensitivities/ritual preferences. I\'m using subtle one-tap preference chips and real inline validation for a crisp, tactile, and immersive experience. I\'m also preparing the design for the primary CTA, which will be a `Confirm Private Consultation` deep midnight navy button with a smooth hover effect. Finally, I\'m developing the bespoke confirmation invitation card, a letterpress-style NOVA Invitation/Boarding Pass.\n\n\n**Adapting Mobile Responsiveness**\n\nOn mobile, the left-hand video canvas morphs into a top header banner with the selected category\'s media, the NOVA logo, a ritual badge, and a close button. Below, the consultation flow is preserved, ensuring no horizontal overflow. I\'ve also implemented step validation and date helpers in `/src/utils/bookingLogic.ts` and their corresponding tests in `/src/utils/bookingLogic.test.ts` for instant user feedback.\n\n\n**Planning Implementation Validation**\n\nI\'m now detailing a comprehensive verification plan. I\'m focusing on rigorous unit tests within `bookingLogic.test.ts` to cover booking step validation, date generation, and ritual preference toggling. The validation will include testing date validity and string formatting, and also test cases for clean appending and removal of notes. I\'ll verify the design and logic across all steps and states for desktop, mobile and tablet.\n\n\n**Verifying End-to-End Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing test cases for comprehensive end-to-end user journey validation across desktop and mobile. I will verify the immersive full-viewport split-sanctuary on desktop, confirming updates to the dossier and that validation alerts appear. I\'ll meticulously test date/time selection, ritual preferences, and confirm the confirmation invitation renders correctly on both desktop and mobile, ensuring there are no console errors.\n\n\n"
