  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Navigation Interaction**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the screenshot provided, specifically focusing on the navigation bar interaction. I\'m paying close attention to every detail that unfolds when hovering over each navigation link. I\'m dissecting the visual cues and responsiveness, aiming to understand the user flow and design nuances. My current efforts are concentrating on identifying patterns and potential improvements within this interaction.\n\n\n**Observing Visual Transition**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual transition effects during hover states. The color shift from white to midnight navy is key. Notably, the introduction of the solid navy bullet point, specifically to the left of \"TREATMENTS\", is crucial. I\'m investigating how this dot smoothly integrates into the existing layout without disrupting the overall alignment. The subtle spacing and size changes during the transition require deeper scrutiny.\n\n\n**Implementing Hover Consistency**\n\nI\'m now focused on applying the crisp midnight navy hover effect consistently across all primary navigation links, including \'BOOK CONSULTATION\'. My primary task is integrating the sharp midnight navy text without the dark drop-shadow on hover. I\'m investigating modifications within `App.tsx` on line 300 to achieve the precise visual clarity observed in the reference image. The goal is a uniform, sharp, and visually impactful navigation experience.\n\n\n**Refining Color Contrast**\n\nI\'m now investigating color contrast, particularly for hover states over the dark video background. I\'m considering using a champagne gold `#E8D5B5` text and dot combination in low-contrast situations, or keeping the ivory dot, to maintain readability. I\'m focusing on ensuring visual accessibility across all scenarios. I need to make sure the ivory `#FBF9F5` is high contrast. I\'m also considering other combinations.\n\n\n**Unifying Active States**\n\nI\'m now integrating the navy bullet dot indicator (`\342\227\217`) for both active and hover states across the navigation. This replacement of the underline creates a unified visual language. I\'m focusing on the `Header.tsx` file for this implementation. I\'m checking that the `InteractiveNovaWordmark` doesn\'t break. I\'m also confirming the midnight navy color, particularly for the \"TREATMENTS\" link in the sticky header, to match the reference images. I\'m going to take screenshots while hovering, to confirm.\n\n\n**Designing Navigation Component**\n\nI\'ve decided to create a reusable `NavHoverLink` component to cleanly handle the hover states across the application. The new `/src/components/NavHoverLink.tsx` is clean, modular, and easy to verify! I\'m carefully designing this component with props like `label`, `onClick`, `isActive`, `variant`, `className`, `ariaExpanded`, and `ariaControls` to ensure flexibility and consistency. I\'m focusing on the visual choreography for each variant, especially ensuring the \"hero\" variant perfectly mirrors `image_30c907b5.png`.\n\n\n**Implementing Hover Detail**\n\nI\'m now implementing the fine details of the hover effect. The key is removing the text shadow for a pure navy color. I\'m focusing on perfectly positioning the navy dot (`\342\227\217`) to the left of the text, ensuring zero layout shift during hover, as seen in `image_30c907b5.png`. Absolute positioning is crucial, and the dot scales and fades beautifully. I\'m integrating this with the light header variant.\n\n\n"
