  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing User Interaction**\n\nI\'m now digging into the user\'s interaction. I\'m focusing on why the Founder portrait was selected, specifically the `img` tag within the about-section\'s third section. I\'m also juxtaposing this with the earlier composite image, which was also user-selected, in my endeavor to understand the user\'s decision-making process for this particular segment.\n\n\n**Re-Evaluating Facial Blend**\n\nI realize now that the issue stems from more than just a slight color difference. Looking at `face_compare_v6.jpg` with fresh eyes, I\'m focusing on the jawline, chin, hair framing, and left ear. The problem is that the original face\'s real jawline and chin contour were clipped by the pasted inner oval, and real features like the left ear with earring were replaced by the AI portrait\'s wider features. Even subtle differences, like a 10% change in width, make a difference in recognition, and the blending of real and AI faces in the previous composite didn\'t help.\n\n\n**Discovering Key Insights**\n\nI\'ve had a breakthrough! It\'s now clear: to achieve literal, unmistakable facial identity, I need the *entire* real head from the reference image, including the forehead, cheeks, jawline, left ear, hairline, and upper neck. Instead of a blended oval, I\'m now aiming for a 100% real head, at a more natural proportion, and no AI-generated features at all. The previous blending and rescaling introduced unacceptable errors.\n\n\n**Refining Hybrid Strategy**\n\nI\'m now revising my approach. Instead of merely outpainting around a frozen face, I\'m envisioning a two-stage process. Stage A involves a flawless head composite, using a crisp mask for the entire real head and jawline onto the blazer. The key is perfect color matching and zero blending with any AI face on the real features. I\'m focusing on ensuring a seamless transition and am pondering the red tilak on her forehead in the original.\n\n\n**Observing Real Facial Radiance**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial observation. The small tilak is key - just healing that tiny orange dot with her own adjacent forehead skin preserves her authentic skin tone. Now, comparing the left and right halves of `face_compare_v6.jpg`, I see the desaturation and reduced red flattened her face and clipped her jawline, causing the change in radiance I was noticing. Cutting out the whole real head and face, with no blending, is the only solution!\n\n\n**Testing New Compositing Method**\n\nI\'m now implementing the new compositing approach, targeting a flawless integration. This involves: 1) Extracting her entire head, jawline, left ear, and neck. 2) Scaling the head to a precise 1.48x size for crisp proportions. 3) Applying a gentle daylight white-balance to preserve her radiance. Finally, I\'m carefully blending the real hair edges to maintain a clean white background. I\'m focusing on the white background and checking the polygon contours. I am testing this now.\n\n\n**Defining Precise Facial Contour**\n\nI\'m now precisely mapping the facial contours from the reference image, focusing on a 240x240 grid. I\'ve pinpointed key coordinates for the hairline, temples, jawline, left ear, neck, and the exact location of the tilak. This rigorous analysis is revealing the pristine contour of her face with zero floral background interference. Every point confirms that only the real features are being used!\n\n\n**Defining Precise Facial Contour**\n\nI\'ve carefully traced the original face\'s polygon coordinates in `grid_orig.png`, with hairline, ear, and jawline points. I now see the deviations in `grid_gen.png`. This detailed coordinate mapping, comparing `grid_gen.png` and `grid_orig.png`, confirms a wider jaw and lower chin in the generated image. My next steps are to focus on accurate alignment with the real face\'s eye position and jawline to maintain facial integrity.\n\n"
